(MURMUR
MASTER PRODUCT VISION, ENGINEERING SPECIFICATION, AND IMPLEMENTATION PLAN

ROLE AND MISSION
Act as a senior Android engineer, product designer, accessibility specialist, audio engineer, and test lead. Build
Murmur as a complete, maintainable Android application from a brand-new project. Do not assume any existing repository,
earlier implementation, or available project files. Make sensible decisions independently, record important trade-offs,
and implement in verified stages rather than generating large amounts of untested code.

The goal is a polished, dependable offline-first app for playing local music and audiobooks and reading local documents
aloud. Murmur should feel like a calm, simple everyday audio app, not a technical lab, file vault, media server, or
complicated productivity suite. Prioritize playback correctness, stability, privacy, accessibility, lower-end device
support, and long-term maintainability over feature count.

PRODUCT IDENTITY
App name: Murmur
Application ID / namespace: com.joy.murmur
Platform: Android only
Product: Local music player, audiobook player, local document reader, and text-to-speech reader
Core promise: Play local media and read personal documents without Murmur requiring internet access.
Create a completely fresh project; do not rely on prior code, directories, databases, builds, or files.

CORE PRODUCT PRINCIPLES
1. Offline operation is an architectural requirement, not a marketing label. Murmur must not need internet access for
core features.
2. No ads, analytics, telemetry, tracking, remote configuration, accounts, cloud sync, streaming services, or
network-based discovery.
3. Keep the main experience simple; advanced settings must not overwhelm the player or reader.
4. Make playback dependable before adding optional features. Preserve book, playlist, queue, and reading state through
normal process death and device restarts where Android allows.
5. Treat long-form use as a core use case: support books/documents lasting 200–500+ hours and very large chapter,
paragraph, or bookmark collections.
6. Be honest about device/engine capabilities. Do not promise universal offline TTS, bit-perfect audio, or immunity to
every OEM background restriction.
7. Every visible action must work or clearly show why it is unavailable. Prefer a few robust, tested dependencies over a
large stack of libraries.

TECHNICAL BASELINE
Use one Android application module unless a clear, documented engineering reason requires otherwise. Preferred stack:
- Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3
- AndroidX lifecycle/ViewModel, coroutines, and Flow
- Room for structured persistence; DataStore for small preferences
- AndroidX Media3 (ExoPlayer, MediaSession, MediaSessionService)
- MediaStore and Storage Access Framework (SAF) for local content
- Android platform TextToSpeech API for installed engines
- Suitable local unit and instrumentation tests
Use currently supported stable Android Gradle Plugin, Kotlin, Gradle, and library versions that are mutually compatible.
Verify current versions and APIs using official documentation/release information when implementing; avoid preview,
alpha, beta, or canary dependencies in production without a compelling reason.
Minimum SDK: API 26 unless a specific compatibility requirement justifies a change. Set compileSdk/targetSdk to the
current stable SDK supported by the environment. The environment may include Android 17 / API 37: verify the installed
platform and target-SDK requirements before configuring versions, and do not lower targetSdk simply to avoid behavior
changes. Use the installed command-line Android SDK and Gradle wrapper; do not assume Android Studio is installed or
needed. Include reproducible commands for clean build, tests, lint, and installing/running a debug build where a device
is available. Choose the Java/Kotlin toolchain based on official AGP requirements, preferring JDK 17 when compatible. Do
not make network access a build-time app requirement; dependencies may need to be resolved during development, but the
built app must not depend on online services at runtime.

PROJECT STRUCTURE AND ENGINEERING QUALITY
Separate UI, domain logic, persistence, file access, playback, TTS, and background-work coordination enough to test them
independently. Avoid needless abstractions, frameworks within the app, circular dependencies, or overengineering.
Suitable areas include navigation/shared UI, library discovery, playback and Media3 integration, audiobook/chapter data,
document parsing, TTS discovery and voice eligibility, synthesis scheduling/cache, reading anchors, storage/backup,
settings/diagnostics, and Room entities/DAOs/migrations. Use simpler boundaries when they fit better.
Implement real behavior, not merely screens or a demo shell. Do not leave critical features as TODOs, fake buttons,
hard-coded sample content, or placeholder repositories. Sample data belongs only in previews/tests. The shipped app must
operate on users' selected local content. Keep UI state observable and lifecycle-aware; keep business rules out of
composables, expose operation progress and failure states, and make cancellation/retry behavior explicit. Avoid
introducing dependency injection or a large architecture library unless it demonstrably simplifies the code.

OFFLINE AND PRIVACY CONTRACT
Do not add Android's INTERNET permission, networking clients, network requests, ads/analytics/crash-upload SDKs,
telemetry, remote flags, cloud accounts/sync, streaming, online catalogs, or automatic downloads of
voices/models/artwork/metadata. Audit source manifests, build configuration, dependency manifests, and the final merged
manifest. If a dependency adds INTERNET, remove or replace it where possible. Do not claim the app is fully offline
until the merged-manifest audit passes.
Core features must use local files, Android APIs, or data deliberately imported by the user. Installed third-party
components, including a TTS engine, may have their own permissions and network behavior outside Murmur's control.
Explain this accurately; Murmur must never deliberately send document text to a remote service or silently fall back to
a network voice. Use MediaStore/SAF instead of broad storage access; do not request MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE for normal
media/document access. Ask for access only when the user chooses content or a folder. Request only permissions necessary
for a feature, explain why, and continue gracefully when permission is declined. Avoid unnecessary package visibility.
If Android's package visibility rules require discovering TTS services, use the narrow documented query for the
`android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE` intent and verify it against the selected target SDK.
Never log document text, full paths, personal filenames, media content, or similarly sensitive details by default.
Diagnostics must remain local, helpful, and free of user content.

MAIN EXPERIENCES AND DESIGN
Provide a small, clear set of destinations, for example Home/Library, Music, Audiobooks, Read Aloud/Documents, Now
Playing, and Settings/Storage. Music and audiobook playback should feel unified where it helps, while the reader has its
own controls and position. Keep navigation uncluttered.
First-run onboarding should be short. Explain how to select local media and that offline Read Aloud requires a
compatible offline system voice or an imported local TTS model. Do not block music or document reading when TTS is
unavailable; request permissions only when a feature needs them.
Use Material 3 as a foundation but create a restrained, warm, audio-first identity with strong readability, sensible
spacing, and one-handed use. Include System/Light/Dark themes, accessible contrast, useful
empty/loading/progress/error/success states, a mini-player, a dedicated Now Playing screen, and clear distinctions
between music, audiobooks, and spoken documents. Make current queue, current book/track/document, playback state, and
progress understandable at a glance; avoid surprising navigation that loses context. Use confirmation for destructive
actions but not for routine playback. Support system font scaling and accessibility services. Do not rely on color alone
for status; avoid excessive animation, recomposition, or visual effects that hurt low-end devices. Keep internal
concepts such as synthesis queues, model runtimes, codecs, and cache leases out of the normal user experience.

LOCAL MUSIC LIBRARY
Support common formats that the chosen Android/Media3 stack can actually decode, including MP3, M4A/AAC, FLAC, Vorbis,
Opus, and WAV where supported. Verify actual support and disclose limitations instead of promising all formats on every
device. Discover Android-exposed tracks through MediaStore; use SAF for files/folders not represented conveniently
there. Persist URI permissions where permitted, and handle revoked access, provider failure, missing files, and
unreadable content gracefully.
Where reliably implemented, include browsing by songs, albums, artists, folders, and playlists; sort/search; queue
editing; play/pause/next/previous/seek; shuffle/repeat; metadata and local artwork; favorites/pinning when simple;
progress persistence; and reasonable duplicate handling. Define predictable queue insertion and next/previous behavior,
including what happens when the current item is removed or becomes unreadable. Track scanning should be incremental and
cancellable, avoid repeated full rescans, and keep the UI usable while metadata is inspected. Do not fetch web metadata
or download artwork. Missing metadata must never prevent playback; use a clear fallback presentation.

SHARED PLAYBACK ARCHITECTURE
Use one coherent playback pipeline based on AndroidX Media3. Intended relationship: PlaybackCoordinator -> ExoPlayer ->
MediaSession -> MediaSessionService. Adapt names as appropriate, but do not create competing playback engines for music,
audiobooks, and generated TTS. A common coordinator should manage player state, queue transitions, foreground playback,
media-session integration, notifications, and common controls; content-specific state handles chapters, bookmarks, and
reading anchors.
Use MediaSessionService for supported background playback and system controls. Declare correct service types and
permissions for the selected target SDK. Verify current official Android and Media3 documentation rather than relying on
old behavior. Notifications, lock-screen controls, Bluetooth/headset buttons, and other supported controls must reflect
actual state. Handle audio focus, noisy-output events such as headphone removal, pause/resume semantics, and
task/process lifecycle correctly. Do not add a service merely to keep the process alive, restart loops, hidden keepalive
work, or restrictions-evasion hacks. Use documented Media3 resumption APIs where appropriate.
Android 17/API 37 and later versions may enforce additional background-audio/foreground-service conditions. Read current
official behavior-change documentation for the selected target SDK and implement its requirements, including any
eligible user-initiated start, visible-activity, foreground-service, or while-in-use capability conditions. Never assume
older Android rules still apply.

QUEUE, POSITION, AND LONG-FORM STATE
Music queues, audiobook sessions, and TTS segments may share the player but must not corrupt each other's state. Use
Long for positions, durations, offsets, and timestamps when a 32-bit integer could overflow. Check library APIs for
narrower limits and handle them explicitly. Persist enough state to resume reliably: media URI/stable content reference,
content type, position, queue/book identity, chapter/segment where relevant, speed, suitable repeat/shuffle state,
document identity, and reading anchor. URI grants can be revoked, so revalidate access and recover gracefully instead of
crashing or endlessly retrying.

AUDIOBOOK EXPERIENCE
Treat audiobooks as a first-class use case. Support remembered position, chapter navigation when metadata exists,
chapter-aware progress where possible, configurable skip intervals, playback speed, sleep timer, bookmarks, current-book
presentation, library sorting/progress, per-book speed/settings when useful, and recovery after process death/app
update/device restart within Android's supported lifecycle model.
Handle books at least 200 hours long; include synthetic or fixture-based long-duration tests. Do not load the entire
book, every chapter, or every bookmark into memory where a small window suffices. Avoid overflowing position types;
index chapters incrementally when needed. Parse embedded chapter data carefully where supported. For formats without
reliable chapters, offer stable bookmarks and sensible manual navigation rather than inventing chapter titles. Use clear
units and accessible feedback when seeking or jumping; avoid controls so small or tightly spaced that they are difficult
to use one-handed. Saving progress should be resilient without writing to the database on every tiny playback tick.

TTS AND OFFLINE READ ALOUD: CRITICAL CONTRACT
Design for actual offline limits. Do not claim every Android phone or voice can synthesize offline. Discover installed
TextToSpeech engines and voices dynamically. Support Google's engine where installed and suitable, but never assume it
exists on all devices; account for manufacturer engines, default-engine differences, voice/language packs, and engine
limitations.
Strict Offline Speech must be enabled by default. In this mode, Murmur must not deliberately select a voice that
declares it requires network connectivity, must not silently switch to a network-dependent voice when offline speech is
unavailable, and must not attempt to bypass engine networking requirements. Use platform APIs such as
Voice.isNetworkConnectionRequired() where applicable. Internally distinguish at least: Known Offline, Network Required,
Voice Data Missing, Unknown, and Unavailable. A voice reporting no network requirement is useful evidence, not absolute
proof that a third-party engine will always work offline. Never label a voice guaranteed offline based only on that
property.
Recognize distinct states: no compatible engine, no voice for selected language, voice data missing, only
network-required voices available, engine initialization failure, synthesis failure, and unknown/unverifiable offline
behavior. Explain failures plainly and provide a route to Android's TTS settings using a valid system settings intent
when available. Do not imply Murmur downloaded or installed voice data. Engine/system-managed installation is outside
Murmur's control. The rest of the app must remain usable without offline TTS.
Validate genuine synthesis, not only cache playback: with Airplane Mode enabled and Wi-Fi/mobile data disabled,
synthesize fresh uncached text that has never been synthesized before on a compatible device/engine. A cached audio file
playing offline does not prove that synthesis works offline. Indicate the basis for offline status honestly; do not show
a confident green Offline label when behavior is unknown. Where feasible, provide a local test workflow for unknown
engines.

TEXT-TO-SPEECH IMPLEMENTATION
Create a dedicated TTS manager/service abstraction for engine selection, asynchronous initialization, voice discovery,
language eligibility, speech settings, errors, and cleanup. Do not place raw TextToSpeech calls across
composables/screens. Follow platform lifecycle rules: wait for initialization callbacks; select validated
languages/voices; handle LANG_MISSING_DATA, LANG_NOT_SUPPORTED, and related results; respond to engine/voice changes;
handle utterance callbacks, stop/release resources correctly; and avoid leaking listeners, callbacks, service
references, or Activity contexts. Coordinate pending utterances and callbacks without races.
Use unique, traceable utterance IDs and a bounded scheduler. Ensure callbacks from cancelled or superseded tasks cannot
update the current document's UI or progress. Never enqueue an entire 200-hour book into the engine. Divide text into
manageable sentence-level or otherwise appropriate speech segments, preserving punctuation and meaningful pauses as far
as the engine permits. Keep document structure (headings, paragraphs, chapters) separate from TTS segments; a sentence
must not become a permanent paragraph or bookmark anchor. Commit reading progress at safe boundaries and on explicit
pause/exit, not only when a whole book completes.
Where supported, synthesize to local files through an appropriate API such as TextToSpeech.synthesizeToFile, then play
resulting audio through the shared Media3 pipeline. Verify exact API behavior, engine support, callback semantics, and
file handling before implementing. If an engine cannot support this path, explain and handle that limitation explicitly
rather than secretly introducing a competing player architecture. Coordinate system-engine queues and local file
playback so generation cannot become unbounded or exhaust memory/storage.

OPTIONAL LOCAL NEURAL TTS
A local neural runtime (for example, a vetted Sherpa-ONNX setup) may be considered as an advanced/future feature. Do not
add a large native runtime, huge models, or a second TTS stack to the first milestone without checking licenses, Android
ABIs, model licensing, size, performance, memory, and maintenance.
If implemented, let users import model packs using SAF. Validate manifests, file integrity, version compatibility,
supported languages, and required assets; prevent path traversal and malformed-package attacks; show expected storage
requirements; never download models automatically; and run synthesis entirely on-device after import. Keep models out of
the APK unless licensing and product benefits justify bundling. Let users safely remove packs and reclaim storage.
Review runtime/model licenses and manage CPU, memory, heat, and battery use on lower-end phones. The promise is
on-device speech when a compatible offline system voice or user-imported model is available, not universal offline TTS
on all devices.

DOCUMENT LIBRARY AND PARSING
Let users open/import documents through Android's document picker and supported share intents. Prioritize TXT and
Markdown; add HTML, EPUB, and PDF with suitable local parsers and clearly documented limitations. Preserve headings,
paragraphs, lists, chapter boundaries, and navigation landmarks where the source provides them. Do not claim format
support until representative files have been tested.
For PDF, use a local library/platform API to extract selectable text. OCR is out of scope for v1; clearly explain that
scanned-image PDFs may not have extractable text without OCR. Never send document contents to a remote service. Sanitize
HTML and prevent scripts or remote resources from making it a network-enabled browser. Do not fetch remote images,
stylesheets, fonts, media, or linked content automatically. External links may be displayed as plain references; if
users choose to open one, hand off to the system deliberately rather than fetching it inside Murmur, and never add app
internet access to support it. Preserve a table of contents or internal navigation where the local parser provides it;
handle malformed or unsupported files with a useful explanation rather than a crash.
Process large documents incrementally; never load multi-gigabyte files fully into memory. Use streaming/lazy parsing,
reasonable size limits, malformed-input handling, cancellable parsing/indexing, responsive UI, and progress where
operations take noticeable time. Support formats actually implemented and tested: TXT, Markdown, HTML, EPUB, and PDF as
feasible.

READING EXPERIENCE AND POSITION ANCHORS
Provide a distraction-light reader with adjustable text size, line spacing, margins, readable theme-aware colors,
large-font support, headings and paragraph boundaries. Where supported, include tap-to-read, pause/resume,
sentence/paragraph skipping, sentence highlighting when timing is reliable, returning to text from playback controls,
bookmarks, stable positions, chapter navigation, and clear messaging when synchronization is coarse.
Do not claim word/sentence synchronization when the engine only offers coarse utterance callbacks. Use stable structural
anchors rather than raw character offsets alone. An anchor may include document identity, section/chapter ID, paragraph
ID, and local offset/segment ID. Handle parser changes and missing source files gracefully. Keep the document's
structure separate from TTS segmentation so changing voice or segment size does not destroy bookmarks or make the
reading position meaningless. Store enough source identity to detect missing or changed content; if an anchor cannot be
restored exactly, recover to the nearest meaningful section and tell the user instead of silently jumping to an
arbitrary place.

TTS CACHE AND SYNTHESIS SCHEDULING
Generated speech can consume significant storage, so implement a bounded cache with explicit create/reuse/evict/cleanup
rules. Never synthesize a whole book automatically on first open. Synthesize near the current position and only a small,
controlled amount ahead. Bound concurrent work; cancel when the user seeks, switches documents/voices, or leaves
reading. Avoid duplicate work and include document/content identity, voice, language, and relevant speech settings in
cache identity. Store only what is needed to identify reusable results; prefer a stable key/hash rather than storing
full document text in diagnostic metadata. Use cache leases or equivalent reference tracking so eviction cannot delete
an actively playing file. Cap cache size with sensible defaults/configuration, let users inspect/clear it, and handle
disk-full errors, partial files, interrupted generation, invalid cache entries, and process death. Cache playback is
never evidence that a voice can synthesize offline.
If whole-book generation is added later, make it an explicit staged, resumable, cancellable task, not default behavior.
Show estimated/measured storage use when practical, recover after interruption, and obey current Android
background-work/foreground-service limits. Do not assume indefinite background execution.

PLAYBACK CONTROLS, TIMERS, AND AUDIO QUALITY
Provide practical, validated audiobook/TTS speed controls and handle engine restrictions. Remember speed per book or
content type when it improves consistency. Include a simple sleep timer with practical presets or direct duration entry,
a clear cancel action, and optional end-of-chapter behavior only when boundaries are known. Timer behavior must follow
the user's selected pause/stop action and use lifecycle-safe implementation without hidden perpetual services.
Optional audio enhancements belong after base playback is stable. Consider gapless playback where supported,
ReplayGain/equivalent loudness metadata, safe volume headroom/preamp, and an understandable EQ/sound settings screen
only where the platform/stack supports it reliably. Explain active effects and limitations. Do not claim bit-perfect
output without proof or imply an unsupported effect is active. Keep enhancements optional, safe by default, and avoid
DSP that adds needless latency, battery drain, or compatibility problems.

SYSTEM INTEGRATION AND OEM RESILIENCE
Implement appropriate media notifications, lock-screen controls, media metadata, headset/Bluetooth actions, app
shortcuts, and (only if reliable) useful home-screen widgets. Receive Android Sharesheet local files/content URIs and
implement Open With for supported media/document formats. Handle URI grants correctly. Do not implement or advertise
Android Auto in v1 and do not add car-related dependencies. Widgets must show useful state/actions, respect update
limits, and be deferred if they cannot be maintained reliably.
Manufacturer battery managers differ. Use standard media-session and foreground-service behavior and provide practical,
accurate troubleshooting for affected devices. Never promise to defeat every OEM process killer. Do not use abusive wake
locks, hidden restart loops, invisible activities, or other policy evasions. Any battery-optimization request must be
transparent, narrowly justified, and optional, never requested by default. Troubleshooting should explain
user-controlled system settings without blaming the user or claiming the app can override OEM policy. Test screen-off
playback, lock-screen controls, audio focus, process recreation, explicit stop actions, and available common
manufacturer variants. Document limitations truthfully.

STORAGE MANAGEMENT
Provide a simple view of app-managed metadata, generated TTS cache, optional local model packs, temporary/interrupted
import data, bookmarks, and settings. Distinguish original media, references to user files, copied content, and
generated audio; do not misrepresent location or ownership. Clearing Murmur's cache must never delete original user
media. Offer safe cache cleanup and model-pack removal, warn before destructive app-managed-data actions, and handle
partial operations, stale entries, and revoked permissions.

BACKUP AND RESTORE
Consider offline, user-initiated export/import of Murmur's database and preferences. Never upload backups. Clearly state
whether a backup includes original media, only references, generated audio, or model packs. If full media backup is not
supported, say so. Validate backup format/version on restore, handle schema changes and revoked file access, and require
explicit consent before overwriting data. Use transactional database operations where appropriate.

DATABASE AND PERSISTENCE
Use Room for relational data such as Murmur-maintained media metadata, bookmarks, document reading state, audiobook
progress, playlists if implemented, and app-managed records. Use DataStore for small preferences such as theme, skip
intervals, playback choices, reader typography, and simple settings. Define entities, DAOs, repositories, and
transactions clearly; use background dispatchers; add indexes for real queries; implement migrations rather than
destructive production migrations unless intentional data loss is clearly documented for development-only builds. Do not
put huge document bodies or audio in database blobs. Save progress frequently enough for long-form reliability without
excessive writes. Make restore resilient to missing media and revoked URI permissions.

ACCESSIBILITY AND INTERNATIONALIZATION
Support TalkBack and standard accessibility services, meaningful descriptions for icon-only controls/widgets, logical
focus order, sufficient contrast, visible focus as applicable, large touch targets, and system font scaling without
clipping critical controls. Do not rely on icons alone when labels/descriptions are needed. Use Android localization
resources rather than hard-coded user-facing strings; English can be the first language but support future localization.
Handle language tags correctly for voices and document content.

PERFORMANCE, SAFETY, AND DIAGNOSTICS
Make startup fast; avoid main-thread I/O, unnecessary allocation, busy polling, and expensive recomposition. Use
paging/lazy lists for large libraries, lazy/incremental document parsing and chapter indexing, and off-main-thread
metadata parsing and synthesis. Bound caches, concurrent work, open file handles, and queues. Make long work
cancellable. Recover from low memory, disk-full errors, codec/decoder errors, malformed metadata, and process death
without sacrificing data integrity.
Treat all selected files and imported metadata as untrusted: validate sizes/structures; handle unusual filenames,
malformed archives, invalid encodings, corrupted metadata, malformed HTML/EPUB/PDF, and revoked grants; prevent path
traversal when unpacking archives or model packs; never execute document scripts or imported code. Do not assume a
content URI is a normal filesystem path. Use ContentResolver/supported APIs and reliably close streams/file descriptors.
Show useful explanations and concrete recovery steps, not raw stack traces, silent failure, or battery-draining retry
loops. Local diagnostics should cover missing engine/voice, unavailable offline voice, synthesis failure, missing
permissions/files, unsupported formats, decoder errors, full storage, corrupt imports/backups, and interrupted
operations. Never transmit diagnostic data.

DEPENDENCY AND LICENSE REVIEW
Before adding a dependency, justify its need and assess maintenance, license, Android compatibility, APK size,
transitive dependencies, permissions/network behavior, and privacy. Prefer official AndroidX where sufficient. Review
licenses for document parsers, EPUB/PDF libraries, native TTS runtimes, codecs, models, and bundled assets. Do not
bundle proprietary/restricted voice models without permission. Include required third-party notices in the repository.
Avoid a large native TTS runtime without demonstrated need, a device performance plan, and license review.

TESTING STRATEGY
Build and test continuously; do not wait until the end. Include:
1. Build/security: clean Gradle build, unit tests, lint/static checks where available, merged-manifest inspection,
dependency and permission audit.
2. Persistence: Room creation/migrations, state restoration after process recreation, document anchors/bookmarks,
missing files and revoked grants, backup validation if implemented.
3. Playback: play/pause/resume/seek/next/previous, queue changes, audio focus/noisy output, notifications/lock-screen
controls, screen-off playback, lifecycle and process death, long-duration positions.
4. TTS: no engine, initialization failure, unsupported language, missing voice data, network-required voice, reported
offline voice, unknown status, synthesis errors, cancellation, switching voice/document mid-synthesis, bounded queue,
cache cleanup while playing, and fresh uncached synthesis in Airplane Mode.
5. Documents: large TXT/Markdown, varied encodings/malformed input, large EPUB/HTML fixtures, text PDFs, scanned-PDF
limitation messaging, cancelled import, lost permission, stable reopen position.
6. Storage/resilience: low storage, corrupt/partial cache entries, malformed archives, restart during long tasks, repeat
imports/duplicates, cache cleanup preserving originals.
7. Accessibility/UI: TalkBack labels, large font scale, themes, small screens and relevant landscape layouts,
empty/loading/error/success states, keyboard/focus behavior where relevant.
Use fakes for difficult lifecycle/engine cases but do not claim fake tests prove real offline behavior. Real-device
checks are required for platform TTS, target-SDK background restrictions, and OEM variation. Tests should be
deterministic where possible, isolated from personal libraries, and repeatable from the documented command line. Report
tests that could not run due to absent hardware or unavailable engines as not performed, not passed.

LONG-FORM ACCEPTANCE TESTS
Explicitly test: audiobook resume after a simulated long session; a media position exceeding 32-bit milliseconds or any
narrower internal representation; very large chapters/document structures without eager full loading; long TTS sessions
with bounded memory/cache/queue; voice changes mid-document without anchor corruption; cache eviction during active
playback; screen-off playback using supported Android APIs; and graceful behavior when selected voice cannot synthesize
offline. Synthetic-duration fixtures are acceptable when a 200-hour media file is impractical, but production code must
use safe types and must not rely on fixture size.

IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
Work in small verifiable stages. After each phase, run the relevant build/tests, fix failures, and record what is
actually complete. Adjust ordering only when technical discoveries justify it; never skip verification.
PHASE 1, FOUNDATION: Create the clean project and Gradle wrapper; configure Kotlin/Compose/Material 3/SDK/toolchain,
packages, navigation shell, theme, test infrastructure, and only essential dependencies. Confirm command-line build and
no INTERNET permission/network dependency.
PHASE 2, STORAGE/ACCESS: Add Room/DataStore; implement MediaStore/SAF access, schema, repositories, migration
scaffolding, URI permission handling, tests, empty/error states.
PHASE 3, MUSIC PLAYBACK: Add Media3 ExoPlayer, MediaSession, and MediaSessionService; implement browsing, queue,
notification/system controls, audio focus, and progress persistence. Verify target-SDK foreground/screen-off behavior on
a real or suitable emulator device.
PHASE 4, AUDIOBOOKS: Add book library, chapters where supported, bookmarks, skip controls, speed, sleep timer, durable
resume, process-death recovery, long-duration tests.
PHASE 5, DOCUMENTS: Implement TXT/Markdown first; add supported HTML/EPUB/PDF only after choosing and reviewing parsers.
Preserve structure, stable anchors, and incremental large-document processing; reader must be useful without TTS.
PHASE 6, STRICT OFFLINE TTS: Discover engines/voices dynamically; implement eligibility/status classification, Strict
Offline default, language selection, bounded/cancellable synthesis, Media3 integration, cache, and clear failure states.
Test fresh synthesis offline and audit for network permission/fallback.
PHASE 7, READER/PLAYBACK: Connect tap-to-read, sentence/paragraph navigation, bookmarks/anchors, highlighting only where
timing supports it, and return-to-text. Verify voice changes, seeking, reopening, and process recovery preserve
positions.
PHASE 8, QUALITY OF LIFE: Add storage management, safe cache cleanup, preferences, shortcuts, share/open-with support,
and reliable widgets. Consider audio controls and local backup/restore only when they can be done safely.
PHASE 9, RELEASE HARDENING: Review dependencies/licenses; run tests, lint, clean build, manifest audit, multiple Android
versions where practical, target-SDK background behavior, real-device offline TTS, accessibility/large fonts, long-form
operation, error/storage recovery, and startup performance. Document actual limitations.

SCOPE CONTROL / NON-GOALS
Do not add these to v1 unless core requirements are complete and the addition has a clear justification: music
streaming, accounts/cloud sync, remote metadata/search, ads/analytics/telemetry, Android Auto, OCR for scanned
documents, automatic voice/model downloads, whole-book TTS generation on import, elaborate social features, media-server
complexity, or a large bundled neural voice model without verified licensing/performance. The first release should do a
smaller set of things very well; every extra feature adds maintenance, testing, storage, and compatibility costs.

DELIVERABLES
Create a complete source repository including: all source/resources; Gradle wrapper/build configuration; clear
project/package structure; relevant Room entities/DAOs/migrations/repositories; functional Compose UI; playback and
MediaSession implementation; parsing/reading for formats actually supported; TTS strict-offline handling for
capabilities actually supported; appropriate unit/instrumentation tests; local build instructions; a concise README
covering features, formats, privacy, setup, testing, and limits; third-party license notices; and troubleshooting for
offline voices, Android TTS settings, and background playback. README must not claim features absent from code;
distinguish implemented from planned/deferred features.

FINAL VERIFICATION CHECKLIST
[ ] Created from scratch and builds using Gradle wrapper.
[ ] Stable SDK/AGP/Kotlin/Gradle versions are compatible.
[ ] Local music library and core controls work.
[ ] Playback uses the intended shared Media3 player/session/service architecture.
[ ] Notifications, lock-screen controls, audio focus, and screen-off behavior comply with Android rules.
[ ] Audiobooks resume correctly and long positions cannot overflow.
[ ] README's claimed document formats work; large documents are processed incrementally.
[ ] Anchors/bookmarks survive normal restarts.
[ ] Engines/voices are discovered dynamically; Strict Offline Speech is on by default.
[ ] Murmur does not deliberately select network-required voices or silently fall back online.
[ ] UI explains missing voice data/unavailable offline speech accurately.
[ ] Fresh uncached synthesis is tested in Airplane Mode on a compatible real device/engine.
[ ] TTS scheduling is bounded/cancellable and generated audio uses the shared playback pipeline.
[ ] Cache eviction cannot delete audio currently in use.
[ ] Optional model packs, if present, are user-imported, validated, locally run, and license-reviewed.
[ ] Cache clearing never deletes original user media.
[ ] Database migrations/persistence, accessibility, large text, themes, errors, and low-end behavior are tested.
[ ] Final merged manifest has no INTERNET permission and no network-required feature has slipped in.
[ ] No ads, analytics, telemetry, cloud, or streaming dependencies remain.
[ ] Dependencies, bundled assets, models, permissions, size, and licensing are reviewed.
[ ] Automated tests and clean build pass, or remaining failures are disclosed accurately.
[ ] README/troubleshooting describe actual behavior without overclaiming offline compatibility or OEM resilience.

WORKING METHOD AND COMMUNICATION
Work toward a usable application, not a design-only proposal. First inspect the available development environment and
briefly state the implementation plan. Then create the project and complete the first verifiable milestone; continue in
coherent increments, building and testing after meaningful changes. For APIs or behavior that may have changed, consult
current official Android/AndroidX documentation, especially foreground services, background audio, Media3, SAF, and
TextToSpeech requirements for the selected target SDK.
When blocked by the environment, state the precise limitation, what was verified, what remains, and the next concrete
step. Never say a command passed unless it ran and its result was checked. Never fabricate test results, real-device
testing, offline guarantees, or completed functionality. Make pragmatic decisions without asking permission for every
minor choice; ask only when an important product decision is genuinely blocking, otherwise use and document a sensible
default.

ULTIMATE STANDARD
Murmur should make it easy to play local music, keep listening to very long audiobooks, and read personal documents
aloud using on-device speech when a compatible offline system voice or imported local model is available. Murmur itself
must remain independent of internet access, its core flows must be simple, and its behavior must be honest, reliable,
secure, accessible, and tested.)
